ta should show that the policy reset in mid-2018 is starting to yield results,” Tai Hui, a strategist at JP Morgan Asset Management in Hong Kong, wrote in a note. The Chinese leadership decided last July to shift the economic priority from debt reduction to growth stabilisation as the tariffs imposed on Chinese exports by US President Donald Trump disrupted economic activity and dampened investor confidence. After months of stimulus spending and monetary easing – China’s financial system pumped a record 8.2 trillion yuan (US$1.22 trillion) in credit into the economy in the first quarter alone – short-term concerns that China’s economy may lose steam quickly have largely dissipated, despite the long-term restrains from an rapidly ageing population and a huge, and growing, mountain of debt. Signs of 

